      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate document carefully.\n\n**Certificate-level info:**\n- Number: R137/2012-A-NL1-20.07 revision 6\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 4031533\n- Page total: 6\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 15 December 2025\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Address lines: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- Elster GmbH, Steinern Straße 19-21, 55252 Mainz-Kastel, Germany\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same as applicant: Elster GmbH (labeled \"Applicant and Manufacturer\")\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Gas meter\" (from R 137-1:2012 \"Gas meters\") — the type is \"Ultrasonic Gas Meter\"\n- Type designations: Q.Sonic^max, Q.Sonic^max6\n- Module designation: Not applicable (or null). The cert doesn't mention module designation. I'll omit or set null. Better to omit since not present. Actually the schema says `module_designation # \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null`. Since not present, omit or null. I'll omit.\n- Description: \"An Ultrasonic Gas Meter, Manufacturers mark: Elster\"\n\n**Model family:**\n- Two model variants: Q.Sonic^max (6 direct paths and 2 reflection swirl paths, accuracy class 0.5 and 1.0) and Q.Sonic^max6 (6 direct paths, accuracy class 1.0)\n- family_name: \"Q.Sonic^max family\" — Table 3 says \"General characteristics of the Q.Sonic max family of instruments\". The family name could be \"Q.Sonic\". I'll use \"Q.Sonic\".\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: R137 (canonical). The rec is R 137-1:2012. Canonical: \"R137\". Edition: hmm. R 137-1:2012 — the edition year is 2012. The schema says edition (int). For R137-1, edition could be 1? Actually \"R 137-1:2012\" means Part 1, 2012 edition. OIML R 137-1:2012 is \"Gas meters – Part 1: Requirements\". The edition field as int — ambiguous. I could put edition: 1 and amendment... Actually maybe edition: 2012? The schema says edition (int). For R60-2017 the edition would be 2017? Hmm. Let me think. In previous examples, \"R 60:2000\" edition might be 2000. The instruction \"edition (int)\" — I think it refers to the year. I'll use 2012. Amendment: null.\n- scheme: A\n- accuracy_classes: The cert states \"0.5; 1.0 for Q.Sonic max, 1.0 for Q.Sonic max6\". So accuracy classes are 0.5 and 1.0. accuracy_classes: [0.5, 1.0].\n\n**Test reports:**\n- NMi-9200288-01 dated 18 January 2012, 48 pages — type evaluation report (the \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\" — the initial one from 2012). Role: type_evaluation_report. The others are revisions/additional tests — role: test_report.\n- NMi-16200535-01 revision 1, 1 September 2017, 35 pages\n- NMi-1902121-01, 12 October 2018, 14 pages\n- NMi-2326650-01, 12 October 2020, 20 pages\n- NMi-2500011-01, 29 June 2021, 21 pages\n- NMi-3567045-01, 13 December 2022, 19 pages\n- NMi-3684021-01, 6 June 2025, 40 pages\n- NMi-3715752-01, 6 June 2025, 15 pages\n\nAlso documentation folder number T10586-5 — \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in documentation folder number T10586-5.\" Role: documentation_file.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- Initial, 2 November 2020, \"-\"\n- 1, 26 August 2021, \"Revision due to new hardware: Field terminal board version BD; Main Board version AJ.\"\n- 2, 18 August 2022, \"Addition of firmware V3.40.\"\n- 3, 13 December 2022, \"New Board (Ethernet Range Extender)\"\n- 4, 11 June 2025, \"Addition of Q.Sonic max6 6-path meters for accuracy Class 1.0 only.\"\n- 5, 14 July 2025, \"Addition of firmware V3.44.\"\n- 6, 15 December 2025, \"Addition of firmware V3.44. (bugfix patch #1)\"\n\n**Footnotes:**\n- [1] \"Basically the Q.Sonic max6 is identical to the Q.Sonic max with the two swirl paths removed.\" (page 2 footnote, applies to path configuration)\n- [1] (page 5 notes) \"If higher values are chosen for Qmin and/or Qt and/or lower values for Qmax, it has to be taken into account that: 5 ≤ Qmax/Qmin < 50: Qmax/Qt ≥ 5; Qmax/Qmin ≥ 50: Qmax/Qt ≥ 10\"\n\nBoth are labeled [1]. I'll include both, maybe as separate entries. Marker \"1)\" vs \"[1]\". I'll use \"[1]\" for both but texts differ. Hmm, the schema footnote marker is a string. I'll include both with marker \"[1]\" — but two entries with the same marker. Maybe distinguish: first footnote on page 2 marker \"[1]\", second on page 5 marker \"[1]\". I'll keep both entries; that's fine.\n\nAlso the notes for tables 3 and 4: \"For tables 3 and 4, the corresponding flow rates can be calculated as follows: Q = V · 1/4 · π · ID² · 3600 = 900 · π · ID² · V\". That's a formula, could go into matrix_tables description or footnote.\n\n**Tables 3 & 4 — flow characteristics by size:**\n\nTable 3 (Q.Sonic max): columns: Diameter size (Inch, DN), ID_min [mm], ID_max [mm], V_min [m/s], V_t [m/s], V_max [m/s]\n\nRows:\n- 4 / DN100: 90, 102.3, 0.49, 2.3, 37.6\n- 6 / DN150: 139, 154.1, 0.29, 2.0, 36.6\n- 8 / DN200: 180, 206.4, 0.29, 2.1, 39.2\n- 10 / DN250: 230, 254.4, 0.29, 2.0, 36.2\n- 12 / DN300: 280, 311.2, 0.30, 1.5, 33.8\n- 14 / DN350: 305, 325, 0.28, 1.5, 33.5\n- 16 / DN400: 350, 393.7, 0.30, 1.5, 33.6\n- 18 / DN450: 387.1, 437.9, 0.24, 2.7, 27.1\n- 20 / DN500: 431.8, 488.9, 0.30, 1.6, 31.1\n- 22 / DN550: 489, 530, 0.28, 1.4, 28.7\n- 24 / DN600: 532.2, 590.9, 0.30, 1.5, 30.4\n- 26 / DN650: 609.2, 640.9, 0.26, 2.8, 28.4\n- 30 / DN750: 730.3, 742.9, 0.25, 2.9, 29.5\n- 36 / DN900: 850.5, 894.9, 0.30, 1.5, 29.8\n\nTable 4 (Q.Sonic max6): rows:\n- 3 / DN80: 58, 85, 0.5 (rowspan 2), 2.2 (rowspan 2), 38.2 (rowspan 7)\n- 4 / DN100: 80, 112, 0.5, 2.2, 38.2\n- 6 / DN150: 120, 165, 0.3 (rowspan 8), 1.8 (rowspan 5), 38.2\n- 8 / DN200: 165, 215, 0.3, 1.8, 38.2\n- 10 / DN250: 205, 270, 0.3, 1.8, 38.2\n- 12 / DN300: 255, 320, 0.3, 1.8, 38.2\n- 14 / DN350: 255, 350, 0.3, 1.8, 38.2\n- 16 / DN400: 325, 400, 0.3, 1.5 (rowspan 3), 33.0 (rowspan 3)\n- 18 / DN450: 365, 450, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 20 / DN500: 405, 500, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- (page 5 continuation) \n- 22 / DN550: 450, 550, 0.3 (rowspan 5), 1.5 (rowspan 5), 33.0 (rowspan 5)\n- 24 / DN600: 490, 600, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 26 / DN650: 605, 650, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 30 / DN750: 695, 750, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 36 / DN900: 850, 905, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n\nWait — the page 5 table is a continuation of Table 4 (Q.Sonic max6)? The page 4 header says \"Table 4 General characteristics of the Q.Sonic max6 family of instruments\" and it ends at 20/500 row. Page 5 starts a new table without a label continuing sizes 22-36. Given Table 4 covers max6 and the max6 table has rowspans extending (Vmax rowspan=7 covering rows 3,4,6 — only 3 rows there), the continuation on page 5 must be Table 4's continuation. Also note the Vmax rowspan=7 in the page 4 table covers only the first 3 rows shown, so the remaining rows continue onto page 5. So yes, page 5 table is the continuation of Table 4.\n\nHmm, but let me double-check the rowspan logic: Page 4 Table 4 rows:\nRow 1: 3/80: 58, 85, [Vmin rowspan2]=0.5, [Vt rowspan2]=2.2, [Vmax rowspan7]=38.2\nRow 2: 4/100: 80, 112\nRow 3: 6/150: 120, 165, [Vmin rowspan8]=0.3, [Vt rowspan5]=1.8\nRow 4: 8/200: 165, 215\nRow 5: 10/250: 205, 270\nRow 6: 12/300: 255, 320\nRow 7: 14/350: 255, 350\nRow 8: 16/400: 325, 400, [Vt rowspan3]=1.5, [Vmax rowspan3]=33.0\nRow 9: 18/450: 365, 450\nRow 10: 20/500: 405, 500\n\nWait, Vmax rowspan=7 covers rows 1-7 (sizes 3 through 14). Then Vt rowspan=5 covers rows 3-7 (6,8,10,12,14). Then row 8 (16/400) starts Vt rowspan3=1.5, Vmax rowspan3=33.0 covering rows 8-10 (16, 18, 20). So within page 4: Vmax for sizes 3,4 = 38.2; sizes 6-14 = 38.2 (continuation of the rowspan 7); sizes 16,18,20 = 33.0.\n\nVmin: sizes 3,4 = 0.5; sizes 6-20 = 0.3.\nVt: sizes 3,4 = 2.2; sizes 6-14 = 1.8; sizes 16-20 = 1.5.\n\nPage 5 continuation (rowspan 5 each): Vmin 0.3, Vt 1.5, Vmax 33.0 for sizes 22-36.\n\nSo Table 4 complete:\n- 3/80: IDmin 58, IDmax 85, Vmin 0.5, Vt 2.2, Vmax 38.2\n- 4/100: 80, 112, 0.5, 2.2, 38.2\n- 6/150: 120, 165, 0.3, 1.8, 38.2\n- 8/200: 165, 215, 0.3, 1.8, 38.2\n- 10/250: 205, 270, 0.3, 1.8, 38.2\n- 12/300: 255, 320, 0.3, 1.8, 38.2\n- 14/350: 255, 350, 0.3, 1.8, 38.2\n- 16/400: 325, 400, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 18/450: 365, 450, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 20/500: 405, 500, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 22/550: 450, 550, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 24/600: 490, 600, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 26/650: 605, 650, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 30/750: 695, 750, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n- 36/900: 850, 905, 0.3, 1.5, 33.0\n\nHmm wait — but should the page 5 continuation actually be part of Table 3 or Table 4? Table 3 (Q.Sonic max) already covers sizes 22-36. So the page 5 table must be the continuation of Table 4. Yes.\n\nNow for matrix_tables: I'll create two matrix tables:\n1. \"Flow characteristics by size — Q.Sonic max (Table 3)\" with columns: inch_size, dn, id_min, id_max, v_min, v_t, v_max; units: [null, \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\", \"m/s\", \"m/s\", \"m/s\"]. Wait DN — DN is dimensionless nominal size (e.g., DN100). Unit null.\n2. \"Flow characteristics by size — Q.Sonic max6 (Table 4)\".\n\nRows with values converted: decimal commas → points. 102,3 → 102.3; 0,49 → 0.49; etc.\n\nFor model_level characteristics: The models here are size variants. Following the R137 example given in the instructions, I should populate model_family.models with model IDs and model_level entries for qmax/qt/qmin. But here the tables give velocities and internal bore, not flow rates directly — flow rates computed via Q = 900·π·ID²·V. I'll include model_level entries for the attributes that vary per size: id_min, id_max, v_min, v_t, v_max. But these vary per size within each family model (Q.Sonic max vs Q.Sonic max6). \n\nHmm — how to structure models? The cert covers 2 instrument types (Q.Sonic max, Q.Sonic max6) each with many sizes. Options:\n- model_id per type+size, e.g., \"Q.Sonic max DN100\", etc. That's 29 models.\n- models as the two types, with sizes as config axis.\n\nThe instruction's R137 example: models = sizes (G1.6, G2.5). Here there are two family tables. I think a reasonable approach: model_family.models with model_id like \"Q.Sonic max\" and \"Q.Sonic max6\", and the size variants handled in matrix_tables plus config_level entries? Or models as size variants with family grouping.\n\nActually, the schema has model_family with models[] each having model_id, label, attributes{}. Maybe:\n\nmodels: 29 entries? That seems heavy but complete. Alternatively, models = [\"Q.Sonic max\", \"Q.Sonic max6\"] and sizes as matrix tables only.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful/faithful. The two \"type designations\" are Q.Sonic max and Q.Sonic max6. Each comes in multiple diameter sizes. The primary model axis is the type designation; sizes are size variants within each.\n\nI'll do: model_family.family_name = \"Q.Sonic\", models = two entries:\n- {\"model_id\": \"Q.Sonic max\", \"label\": \"Q.Sonic max\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": [0.5, 1.0], \"path_configuration\": \"6 direct paths and 2 reflection swirl paths\"}}\n- {\"model_id\": \"Q.Sonic max6\", \"label\": \"Q.Sonic max6\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": [1.0], \"path_configuration\": \"6 direct paths\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\"]}}\n\nHmm, attributes values should probably be structured objects too? The schema says attributes{}. For model attributes, I can use structured value objects. Let me keep it reasonable: attributes with structured values.\n\nActually, accuracy class per model: Q.Sonic max: 0.5; 1.0. Q.Sonic max6: 1.0. I could represent as {\"value\": \"0.5; 1.0\"} hmm. The strict value shape applies to \"characteristic value\". For model attributes, I'll use similar structured form for consistency.\n\nThen for the size-dependent data, use config_level with axis \"diameter_size\" per model? The config_level shape: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value, unit_symbol}]}. I could emit config_level entries like:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"minimum_internal_bore\", \"axis\": \"diameter_size (Q.Sonic max)\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"DN100\", \"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"}, ...]}\n\nThat would be 5 attributes × 2 families = 10 config_level entries, each with 14-15 values. That's thorough but duplicates matrix_tables. The instructions say matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables; model_level for attribute×model matrix. The R137 example populated both matrix_tables AND model_level. Here with 29 models, model_level would be huge.\n\nGiven the cert itself designates two instrument types and size tables, I think the cleanest is:\n- model_family.models: 2 entries (Q.Sonic max, Q.Sonic max6) with attributes (path configuration, accuracy class).\n- matrix_tables: two tables (Table 3, Table 4 incl. page-5 continuation).\n- config_level: optionally emit per-size velocity/bore characteristics as config axis \"diameter_size\". I'll include config_level entries for v_min, v_t, v_max, id_min, id_max per family. Hmm, that's a lot of duplication. But the guidance emphasizes extracting matrices into model_level. Let me use config_level with axis combining family and size? E.g. axis: \"diameter_size\", and condition \"Q.Sonic max DN100\"? That conflates.\n\nAlternative: treat each size variant as a model within the family: models = 29 entries: \"Q.Sonic max DN100\" ... \"Q.Sonic max6 DN900\". And model_level entries for id_min, id_max, v_min, v_t, v_max with values per model. This matches \"R137 example\" pattern best (G1.6, G2.5 models). 29 models × 5 attributes = large but valid.\n\nHmm. The example in the rules: R137 gas meter with sizes G1.6/G2.5 — models were sizes. Here the analog is sizes DN80...DN900 within each type. But we have two separate families with different tables.\n\nDecision: I'll use model_id like \"Q.Sonic max DN100\", \"Q.Sonic max DN150\", ..., \"Q.Sonic max6 DN80\", etc. 14 + 15 = 29 models. Then model_level entries for the five attributes. Also keep matrix_tables for the two tables. This is the most complete and consistent with the guidance.\n\nActually wait — is that over-engineering? The instruction says \"model_level[]: attribute × model matrix\". And matrix detection is \"MOST IMPORTANT\". I'll go with the full 29-model approach. Each model entry: {\"model_id\": \"Q.Sonic max DN100\", \"label\": \"DN100 (4 inch) Q.Sonic max\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": ..., \"path_configuration\": ...}}? Hmm, that repeats family attributes. Maybe simpler: model entries just model_id and label; family-level attributes (path config, accuracy class) go into type_level characteristics? No — accuracy class differs per model (max: 0.5;1.0 vs max6: 1.0).\n\nLet me structure:\n\nmodel_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"Q.Sonic\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"Q.Sonic max\", \"label\": \"Q.Sonic max (6 direct paths and 2 reflection swirl paths)\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": [0.5, 1.0], \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}, \"path_configuration\": {\"value\": \"6 direct paths and 2 reflection swirl paths\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}}},\n    {\"model_id\": \"Q.Sonic max6\", \"label\": \"Q.Sonic max6 (6 direct paths)\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": [1.0], ...}, \"path_configuration\": {\"value\": \"6 direct paths\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\"]}}}\n  ]\n}\n\nHmm, accuracy class as list of numbers... value can be number | {min,max} | string. A list isn't in the allowed shape. \"0.5; 1.0\" — that's two class options. Maybe represent as string \"0.5; 1.0\" or as list. Strictly, value shape is number|range|string. I'll use string \"0.5 and 1.0\" or just \"0.5; 1.0\". For max6: \"1.0\". These are in model attributes, so slight flexibility is fine. I'll use strings.\n\nThen the size variants: config_level with axis \"diameter_size\" split per family. Entries like:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_velocity\",\n  \"axis\": \"diameter_size — Q.Sonic max (Table 3)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN100 (4 in)\", \"value\": 0.49, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n\nFive attributes × 2 families = 10 entries. Values are structured objects; condition strings; footnote markers [1] on v_min, v_t, v_max values (both tables have Vmin[1], Vt[1], Vmax[1]; Table 3 Vt also [2]? Wait Table 3 header: V_t^{[1],[2]} — yes Table 3 Vt has footnotes [1] and [2]! But footnote [2] text... Page 5 Notes section lists \"[1] If higher values are chosen...\" — where's [2]? Looking at the markdown: \"Notes: [1] If higher values are chosen for Qmin and/or Qt and/or lower values for Qmax, it has to be taken into account that: - 5 ≤ Qmax/Qmin < 50: Qmax/Qt ≥ 5 - Qmax/Qmin ≥ 50: Qmax/Qt ≥ 10\". Only [1] is defined. [2] is referenced in Table 3 header (V_t^{[1],[2]}) but its definition isn't in the extracted text. I'll include footnote_markers [\"1)\",\"2)\"] hmm — the markers in markdown are \"[1]\", \"[2]\". I'll record footnote markers as \"[1]\" and \"[2]\". But marker format in examples: \"footnote_markers: [\\\"1)\\\"]\". The rules example uses \"1)\". I'll use \"[1]\" as it appears. Either fine; I'll use \"[1]\" matching source. Actually example in rule 7: `{\"value\": ..., \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\"]}`. The markdown shows [1]. I'll normalize to \"1)\"? Hmm. Safer to keep as in source: \"[1]\". I'll go with \"[1]\" and \"[2]\".\n\nFootnotes array:\n- {\"marker\": \"[1]\", \"text\": \"Basically the Q.Sonic max6 is identical to the Q.Sonic max with the two swirl paths removed.\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"[1]\", \"text\": \"If higher values are chosen for Qmin and/or Qt and/or lower values for Qmax, it has to be taken into account that: 5 ≤ Qmax/Qmin < 50: Qmax/Qt ≥ 5; Qmax/Qmin ≥ 50: Qmax/Qt ≥ 10.\"}\n\nTwo entries with same marker — maybe disambiguate by adding context: marker \"[1] (page 2)\" and \"[1] (page 5)\". Hmm, that changes the marker. The schema doesn't forbid duplicates. I'll add a note... Actually to be safe and clear, I'll use markers \"[1]\" (path configuration footnote) and \"[1]\" again — but then consumers can't tell which applies where. The footnote markers on values are per-table anyway (Vmin[1] in tables 3&4 refers to page-5 note). The page-2 [1] refers to path configuration. I'll include both entries; the text distinguishes them.\n\n**type_level characteristics (general, Table 1):**\n- destined_for_measurement_of: \"Gas volume\"\n- environmental_classes: \"M1 / E2\" (condensing humidity). Could split: environmental_class_mechanical: \"M1\", environmental_class_climatic: \"E2\". I'll do environmental_class: {value: \"M1 / E2\"} plus maybe a note. Keep as one: environmental_classes: {value: \"M1 / E2\", ...} with extra note \"Condensing humidity\" — maybe as separate characteristic \"humidity\": \"condensing\". I'll include environmental_classes value \"M1 / E2\" and a characteristic humidity_condition: \"Condensing humidity\"? Simpler: environmental_classes: \"M1 / E2 (condensing humidity)\"? Rules say keep it clean. I'll emit two: \"mechanical_environmental_class\": \"M1\", \"climatic_environmental_class\": \"E2\", and \"humidity\": \"condensing humidity\". Hmm, is that fabrication? It's interpretation but standard (OIML D11 classes M1, E2). I'll keep environmental_classes: \"M1 / E2\" and humidity: \"Condensing humidity\". Good compromise.\n\n- accuracy_class: This varies per model — model_level? Q.Sonic max: \"0.5; 1.0\", Q.Sonic max6: \"1.0\". I'll put accuracy_class in model_level with two entries:\n  {\"attribute\": \"accuracy_class\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": \"Q.Sonic max\", \"value\": \"0.5\"}, {\"model\": \"Q.Sonic max\", \"value\": \"1.0\"}, {\"model\": \"Q.Sonic max6\", \"value\": \"1.0\"}]}\n  Hmm, value strings \"0.5\" — should be numbers? Accuracy class 0.5 is numeric-ish. Value shape allows number. But two classes for max... I could emit two entries in values for the same model. I'll use value 0.5 and 1.0 as numbers, with max appearing twice. Acceptable.\n\n- path_configuration: model_level:\n  {\"attribute\": \"path_configuration\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"Q.Sonic max\", \"value\": \"6 direct paths and 2 reflection swirl paths\"}, {\"model\": \"Q.Sonic max6\", \"value\": \"6 direct paths\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"[1]\"]}]}\n  Value as string is fine (not numeric).\n\n- working_pressure: The Table 1 text: \"When using a preset value, the maximum operating range for pressure p_max / p_min is 6,25 symmetrically divided around the preset pressure.\" Hmm — \"6,25\" — this is odd. Likely it's a ratio pmax/pmin ≤ 6.25? Hmm wait, could be \"6:25\"? Hmm. Actually I don't think it's bar. The text: \"maximum operating range for pressure p_max / p_min is 6,25\" — probably a ratio of 6.25 (hmm, could be \"6,25\" meaning 6.25). Hmm wait maybe it means pmax = 6 to 25? No... Hmm, but actually this doesn't quite make sense either. Let me reconsider: hmm wait, maybe the original says \"the maximum operating range for pressure pmax/pmin is 6:25\"? Or \"1:6,25\"? Hmm. Hmm, I think it means pmax/pmin ratio... hmm, actually maybe the operating pressure range ratio is 6.25? Hmm, like preset pressure × 2.5 and / 2.5 gives ratio 6.25. Yes! \"6,25\" = 6.25 ratio (2.5² = 6.25, symmetric around preset). So: operating_pressure_ratio: value 6.25, dimensionless. I'll record: \"max_pressure_ratio_pmax_over_pmin\": {\"value\": 6.25, \"unit_symbol\": null} with description. Actually to be careful, I'll record as \"working_pressure_range_ratio\": {\"value\": 6.25} and maybe include the descriptive text as a note. Since we don't have a free-text slot in type_level beyond value, I'll just include the numeric with attribute label \"working_pressure_max_operating_range_ratio_pmax_over_pmin\". Good enough.\n\nHmm, wait. Rule 1 says canonical labels, no LaTeX, snake_case. OK.\n\n- ambient_temperature_range: varies by configuration: gas meter: -40…+70 °C; gas meter with EVCD: -40…+55 °C. This is config_level with axis \"EVCD\" (equivalent... EVCD = electronic volume conversion device). \n  config_level entry: {\"attribute\": \"ambient_temperature_range\", \"axis\": \"configuration\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"gas meter\", \"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 70}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}, {\"condition\": \"gas meter with EVCD\", \"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}]}\n\n- gas_temperature_range: similarly: gas meter: -40…+100 °C; with EVCD: -30…+80 °C.\n\n- orientation: \"Horizontal; vertical up; vertical down and all orientations\" — value string.\n- power_supply_voltage: 18–30 V DC → value {min:18, max:30}, unit \"V\", plus power_supply_type: \"DC\".\n\n**Installation conditions** (config_level axis \"flow disturbance\"):\n- upstream_straight_pipe_length: mild: Q.Sonic max ≥5D; Q.Sonic max6 ≥3D + FC + 5D or ≥6D. severe: max: ≥5D + FC + 5D; max6: ≥3D + FC + 5D. These are strings. config_level:\n  {\"attribute\": \"upstream_straight_pipe_length\", \"axis\": \"flow disturbance / model\", \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Q.Sonic max, mild disturbances\", \"value\": \"≥5D\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Q.Sonic max, severe disturbances\", \"value\": \"≥5D + FC + 5D\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Q.Sonic max6, mild disturbances\", \"value\": \"≥3D + FC + 5D or ≥6D\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Q.Sonic max6, severe disturbances\", \"value\": \"≥3D + FC + 5D\"}\n  ]}\n- outlet_piping: \"≥3D\" → type_level: {\"attribute\": \"outlet_straight_pipe_length\", \"value\": \"≥3D\"}. Value string with ≥ — fine as string.\n- inlet pipe diameter tolerance ±3%: {\"attribute\": \"inlet_pipe_diameter_tolerance\", \"value\": 3, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"} — ±3%. Value could be string \"±3%\". I'll do value 3, unit \"%\", hmm ± lost. I'll keep value: \"±3%\"? Rules: numbers not strings... but ±3% is not cleanly a number. I'd say value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\", and label \"maximum inlet_pipe_diameter_tolerance\". I'll do that.\n- temperature sensor location: 2–5D from outlet → {\"attribute\": \"temperature_sensor_installation_distance\", \"value\": {min: 2, max: 5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"D\"}. Unit \"D\" (diameters). unit_symbol \"D\".\n\nMaybe keep installation stuff lighter — but including key ones is good.\n\n**Components (interchangeable components):** \"The ultrasonic transducers and the electronics can be replaced with units of the same type...\" — not specific type designations. So components[] — the schema says include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies. The cert mentions ultrasonic transducers, electronics, pressure sensor (optional). No type designations though. I could add components entries: role \"ultrasonic transducer\", type_designations [] — hmm, empty designations is weak. Rule 8: don't fabricate. I'll skip components or include with type_designations empty? I think skip is safer. Actually maybe include a note in certified_type.description. I'll skip components.\n\n**Flow conditioner types:** CPA50 type A, NOVA 50E — these are permitted accessories. Could go into components with alternatives \"OR\": role \"flow conditioner\", type_designations [\"CPA50 type A\", \"NOVA 50E\"], alternatives \"OR\". That's a genuine component alternative. I'll include it.\n\n**Software table (Table 2):** Software versions with checksums. This is a multi-dim table — put into matrix_tables as \"Software identification\" with columns software_part, version_1, checksum_1, version_2, checksum_2? The table has 4 version/checksum column pairs. Rows contain multiple versions per cell (line breaks). This is messy. Options: matrix_table with columns [\"software_part\", \"versions_and_checksums\"] where the value is a combined string? Or flatten: each software part has a list of {version, checksum} pairs. I'll do matrix_table with columns: [\"software_part\", \"software_version\", \"checksum\"] and one row per version/checksum pair, adding a \"column_group\" maybe (the table seems to have two column groups, maybe per software generation). Hmm, the two groups: versions \"01.00.06\" etc. vs \"01.00.08\" — actually looking closer, group 1 and group 2 might correspond to different hardware? Not stated. I'll just flatten with a \"variant\" column? There's no stated distinction. I'll flatten: columns [\"software_part\", \"software_version\", \"checksum\"], rows: one per pair. NGQFB: (01.00.06, 0x8A36A8B7), (01.00.07, 0xB7568107), (01.00.08, 0x350616D6), (01.00.09, 0x06BDB754). Wait careful: columns are \"Software version | Checksum | Software version | Checksum\". Row NGQFB: 01.00.06/0x8A36A8B7, 01.00.07/0xB7568107, 01.00.08/0x350616D6, 01.00.09/0x06BDB754. NGQMB: 01.00.08/0x81905758, 01.00.10/0x0C432F97, 01.00.12/0xD8615DF7, 01.00.13/0x426E6956. Basic system: 02.08.00.0029/0xEBF74980, 02.10.01.0003/0x7C01880E, 02.11.00.0018/0x75574588, 03.02.00.0000/0x36B6BE1E, 03-40-A/0x0EA0B879, 03-44-A/0xCE9961F4. Density & Viscosity: 02.04.00.0000/0x804BDA24, 02.04.00.0001/0xB887257F, 02.04.00.0005/0x1DF2525D, 03.00.00.0000/0xE062B4B9, 03-01-A/0xA5C2AE67, 03-02-B/0xC3614274. Geometry Correction: 02.04.00.0000/0x90D4BD88, 02.04.00.0001/0xD8D14A5F, 02.05.00.0000/0x6C1DF991, 03.00.00.0000/0x4F26E00E, 03-01-A/0xFD14CF91, 03-02-B/0xD84D93A5. USM: 02.08.00.0000/0x57D99098, 02.10.00.0000/0x3DE0303F, 02.11.00.0018/0x9B8E6234, 03.02.00.0000/0x46340214, 03-03-A/0xC1006EAD, 03-05-A/0x95DB58DB. Gas Quality(★): 02-09-B/0x31D9C769, 02-09-C/0xC83315E2, 02-09-D/0xBD083158, 03-13-A/0x98B1FD23, 03-15-F/0x05CB4161, 03-18-A/0x0E781C1F. Flow Conversion(★): 02-07-C/0x5A084FC6, 02-07-D/0x587979DE, 02-07-E/0xC960A68C, 03-10-D/0xC2172756, 03-14-C/0x3BE626FB, 03-14-F/0x6D579C63. Postprocessing: 02-08-B/0x2C4B20EC, 02-08-C/0x459B14C1, 02-08-D/0x65397437, 03-09-D/0x816A45E3, 03-12-E/0xDBEB2D99, 03-14-A/0x1602675F. Archive: 02-06-A/0xB9652A43, 02-07-A/0x420652AC, 02-07-B/0x013C7919, 03-10-D/0xB6E506A1, 03-10-P/0x384639C5, 03-11-C/0x9909DD67. Modbus: 02-20-A/0x9221E790, 02-20-B/0xF4348ED8, 02-20-C/0xF20B85FE, 03-12-B/0x8E1DD57B, 03-17-A/0x47A64C44, 03-19-B/0x6869AD67.\n\nThat's a lot of rows but fine. Add footnote star (★): \"These