lity, \"R137-1\" is the actual recommendation identifier (Part 1). Hmm... OIML R 137-1 is indeed a distinct publication. I'll go with \"R137\" as canonical id and note... no way to note. Let me just use \"R137-1\"? The instruction: id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"). The canonical form strips \"OIML\" prefix and spaces. So \"R137-1\" seems right. But earlier they mention R137 in the schema example with id R137 for a gas meter cert (\"R137 example\"). Their example extraction shows \"Flow characteristics by size\" without recommendation field example. I'll go with \"R137-1\" to be faithful. Hmm, risk either way; \"R137-1:2012\" is the exact reference. I'll choose \"R137\" — actually no. Let me think about downstream: canonical ids like R60, R137, R49. The \"-1\" part suffix is part of the official title \"R 137-1 (H1) Gas meters Part 1: Metrological and technical requirements\". Downstream systems likely key on \"R137\". But faithfulness... The certificate says R 137-1:2012. I'll use \"R137\" with edition 2012, and since there's no part field, maybe include part in id? I'll go \"R137-1\". Hmm.\n\nDecision: id: \"R137-1\", edition: 2012. Wait but \"edition (int)\" — 2012 is the year. Some might argue R137-1 has Edition 2012 = the edition year. Yes, edition: 2012.\n\nActually hold on, let me reconsider: maybe safer \"R137\"? The graders might expect canonical \"R137\". The example in the prompt's R137 mention (\"R137 example\") uses gas meters. I'll go with \"R137-1\"... ugh. Let me look at rule 1 examples: all about labels not ids. Rule for recommendation: 'id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\")'. Applying same transform to \"R 137-1\" → \"R137-1\". I'm confident in \"R137-1\".\n\n**test_reports roles:** The first report NMi-9200288-01 (18 Jan 2012) is the Type Evaluation Report (\"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\"). The others (2017-2025) are additional/revised test reports — these likely are revised TERs too. Roles: I'll mark first as \"type_evaluation_report\" and the rest as \"test_report\". Documentation folder T10586-5 → \"documentation_file\".\n\nAlso revision history descriptions:\n- Initial: \"-\"\n- \"1\": date 26 August 2021 → \"2021-08-26\", changes \"Revision due to new hardware: Field terminal board version BD; Main Board version AJ.\"\n- \"2\": 2022-08-18, \"Addition of firmware V3.40.\"\n- \"3\": 2022-12-13, \"New Board (Ethernet Range Extender).\"\n- \"4\": 2025-06-11, \"Addition of Q.Sonic max6 6-path meters for accuracy Class 1.0 only.\"\n- \"5\": 2025-07-14, \"Addition of firmware V3.44.\"\n- \"6\": 2025-12-15, \"Addition of firmware V3.44 (bugfix patch #1).\"\n\nrevision as string: \"Initial\", \"1\", ..., \"6\".\n\n**model_level entries:** For the 29 size models approach — let me reconsider scope. Emitting 5 model_level attributes × 29 models each = 145 value objects. Plus two matrix_tables with same data. It's heavy but the instructions explicitly want both (R137 example shows both matrix_tables AND model_level). OK, let's do it. Model IDs: \"Q.Sonic max DN100\" etc. For Table 3 (max): DN100, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN550, DN600, DN650, DN750, DN900 (14). For max6: DN80, DN100, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN550, DN600, DN650, DN750, DN900 (15). Total 29.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n1. minimum_internal_bore (id_min), unit mm, 29 values.\n2. maximum_internal_bore (id_max), unit mm, 29 values.\n3. minimum_flow_velocity (v_min), unit m/s, 29 values, footnote [1].\n4. minimum_transitional_flow_velocity (v_t), unit m/s, 29 values, footnote [1] (max) and [1],[2] for max? Table 3 Vt: [1],[2]. Table 4 Vt: [1]. So footnote markers per value: max rows: [\"[1]\",\"[2]\"]; max6 rows: [\"[1]\"].\n5. maximum_flow_velocity (v_max), unit m/s, 29 values, footnote [1].\n\nThat's a lot of JSON but let's write it carefully.\n\nWait — also model_family.models: should they be the 29 size variants or the 2 types? If models are the 29 size variants, then where do the 2 types live? certified_type.type_designations covers both. And model_family.models would be 29 entries with attributes indicating type, accuracy class, path config. That works: each model entry gets attributes {instrument_type, accuracy_class, path_configuration}. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, models = 2 types, and config_level for sizes. The rule 5 example: \"2-sensor-row vs 4-sensor-row setups\" → config axis. Size variants in gas meters per the R137 example are models though (G1.6, G2.5). Here with two families × sizes, I think models = 29 size variants is most consistent with the R137 example. Each model: {\"model_id\": \"Q.Sonic max DN100\", \"label\": \"Q.Sonic max, DN100 (4 in)\", \"attributes\": {\"instrument_type\": {...}, \"accuracy_class\": {...}, \"path_configuration\": {...}}}. That repeats accuracy/path config 14/15 times. To reduce repetition, I can put family-level attributes only on... hmm, models[] entries each have attributes{}; repetition is acceptable but verbose. Alternatively keep models[] minimal (model_id, label) and put accuracy/path config in model_level entries keyed by model id... no — model_level is for varying attributes. Accuracy class varies between max and max6 but not within. Put it in type_level? No — type_level is whole-cert single value.\n\nPragmatic solution: models[] = 29 entries with model_id including family; attributes omitted for brevity? Schema: models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}} — attributes optional-ish (\"attributes{}\" no comment). I'll include attributes on each model: {\"family\": \"Q.Sonic max\", \"accuracy_class\": ..., \"path_configuration\": ...}? That's 29 × 3 attrs. Hmm heavy but complete. \n\nAlternative cleaner: models[] = 2 family-level entries won't match model_level keys \"Q.Sonic max DN100\". Consumers cross-reference via matrix_tables anyway.\n\nLet me go middle path: model_family.models = 29 entries with model_id \"Q.Sonic max DN100\" etc., label